VITE_APP_NAME="Owe System Stability"
VITE_APP_ENV=production

# Backend reached same-origin through the /api proxy (vercel.json rewrite).
VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api

# SSE is the live transport, BUT on Vercel we keep the REST poller because
# Vercel can't reliably hold a long-lived SSE stream through its rewrite proxy,
# and an https page can't open an http:// EventSource (mixed content). On a
# stream-capable host (nginx/direct), drop this line and set VITE_SSE_URL below.
VITE_STATUS_TRANSPORT=rest
VITE_STATUS_POLL_MS=30000

# SSE endpoint (used when VITE_STATUS_TRANSPORT is not 'rest').
# Same-origin via a reverse-proxy is required on https hosts to avoid mixed content.
VITE_SSE_URL=/sse/status

# ── Credential manager (owe-stability-service) ──────────────────────────────
# Reached same-origin through the /stability rewrite in vercel.json, which
# forwards to http://149.28.112.32:28753/api/owe-stability-service/*.
#
# Why not the absolute backend URL, as local dev uses? An https page is not
# allowed to call an http:// origin (mixed content) — the browser blocks it
# before the request leaves the tab. The backend now sends CORS (`*`), so the
# ONLY remaining blocker is that it answers on http://, not https://.
#
# ── To call the backend DIRECTLY (no Vercel proxy) ──
# Requires the backend on HTTPS (e.g. `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:28753`,
# or nginx + a cert — see DEPLOY.md). Once it has an https URL, delete the
# /stability line below and uncomment this one, pointing at that host:
#
#   VITE_STABILITY_SERVICE_URL=https://<backend-https-host>/api/owe-stability-service
#
# That is the entire frontend change — the app then shows and calls that URL
# directly, exactly like local. Until then, keep the proxy line active:
VITE_STABILITY_SERVICE_URL=/stability

# Public half of the RSA-OAEP keypair that encrypts credential secrets in the
# browser, and the SAME key the local .env uses — a secret encrypted here has to
# be openable by the one private key that exists.
#
# Committing it is correct, not an oversight: a public key is designed to be
# handed out, it can only encrypt, and it is compiled into the JavaScript bundle
# and readable by anyone regardless. Keeping it blank here is what left the
# deployed create form disabled with "No encryption key is configured", because
# .env is gitignored and never reaches the build host.
#
# A VITE_CREDENTIAL_PUBLIC_KEY set in the host's dashboard overrides this line
# (Vite prioritises process.env over .env files), which is the way to rotate the
# key without a commit.
#
# The private half is blanked BELOW rather than merely omitted. Vite loads .env
# first and .env.production on top, so a key left out here does not stay out —
# it inherits from .env and is compiled into the public bundle. Setting it empty
# is what actually keeps it out of a production build. (vite.config.ts also fails
# the build outright if it is non-empty, so this cannot regress silently.)
#
# This is why the admin "copy secret" action cannot work in production until
# decryption moves behind an authenticated Cloudflare Worker route.
VITE_CREDENTIAL_PRIVATE_KEY=
VITE_CREDENTIAL_PUBLIC_
